INT. MIDNIGHT BOWLING ALLEY - NIGHT

Lane 14. Neon pins. The ball return coughs. A scoreboard overhead flickers and shows two names: RICO and BLANK.

RICO VALDEZ, 40s, a heavy man in a purple league shirt, sits on the bench retying his shoes. His ball is a scuffed black orb with initials in gold. He has bowled a perfect game twice in his life, and tonight he is eleven frames in.

On the lane beside him, no one. The scoreboard says BLANK. The seat is empty.

He rises, takes the ball, steps to the line. He breathes. Rolls. The ball hooks gently and blasts the pocket. Strike. The scoreboard dings.

He does not celebrate. He walks to the ball return and waits.

A shadow falls across the approach. A figure stands in the doorway of the lane, small, a tiny silhouette. A girl, maybe nine, in a pink hoodie, holds a ball two sizes too heavy for her.

The girl is DAISY MORALES, and she has been looking at him since the ninth frame.

RICO
Closed to the public.

DAISY
I'm not the public.

RICO
Who are you, then?

DAISY
The next frame.

RICO
That's not a thing.

DAISY
It is on your scoreboard.

He turns. The scoreboard above reads BLANK. As he watches, the letters rearrange. DAISY.

RICO
Somebody typed that.

DAISY
Somebody did.

RICO
Who?

DAISY
My dad. He said you'd show me how.

RICO
Your dad.

DAISY
He bowled here. Before.

RICO
(slowly)
Before what?

DAISY
Before you took the last frame.

Rico stares. The ball return rumbles. His ball rolls up and bumps against his hand, and he does not pick it up.

RICO
Kid. What's your father's name?

DAISY
Manuel.

RICO
Manny Morales.

DAISY
Yes.

RICO
Manny's dead.

DAISY
I know.

RICO
He had a seven-ten split in the tenth. Twenty-three years ago. I bowled against him.

DAISY
He said you let him have it.

RICO
I didn't let him.

DAISY
He said you did.

RICO
He missed. He missed the seven.

DAISY
He didn't miss. You moved the pin.

Rico laughs, a dry bark. Then he stops.

RICO
Who told you that?

DAISY
My dad. In a letter. He wrote it before.

RICO
Before.

DAISY
He wanted me to ask you why.

Rico sinks onto the bench. The neon buzzes. Daisy walks the length of the lane toward him, ball dragging, until she stands in front of him.

RICO
Because I was twenty-two, kid. Because I wanted to be the guy who bowled a three hundred in the state final. And he was better, and I knew it.

DAISY
So you cheated.

RICO
I did.

DAISY
Did you win?

RICO
I won.

DAISY
Did you bowl three hundred?

RICO
(barely audible)
No. Two seventy-one.

DAISY
Dad said you'd say two seventy-one.

Silence. Rico looks at her, at the ball, at the empty lane.

RICO
What do you want from me?

DAISY
Show me how to throw.

RICO
That's all?

DAISY
That's all.

He rises. He places her fingers in the holes. He adjusts her wrist.

RICO
Don't look at the pins. Look at the arrow.

DAISY
Which arrow?

RICO
The one in the middle.

She rolls. The ball wobbles down the lane, drifts left, and drops into the gutter. Daisy deflates.

DAISY
That's bad.

RICO
That's a start.

DAISY
Again?

He fetches her ball. She rolls. It strikes three pins. She jumps. Rico, for the first time in the scene, smiles, and the scoreboard dings.
